      "writtenOn": "2026-02-12T18:19:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think we have this guarantee. Consider the following scenario:\n\n1. Push a very large clip A on to the stack\n2. Push another clip B on to the stack.\n3. Draw 1 is fully contained in A but overlaps with B, so we call `updateForDraw` on B and set its `fDeferredLayer`.\n4. Draw 2 is not fully contained in A and is affected by both A and B, so we call `updateForDraw` on both elements, seeing the `fDeferredLayer` from #3 for B and getting a new layer for A.\n5. Depending on the order we are iterating over `effectiveElements`, we might call `updateForDraw` on A first, so `prevLayer` would be null. This is fine, the backwards search should still stop at B\u0027s `fDeferredLayer`. However, then we call `updateForDraw` on B later and currently unconditionally update `prevLayer` to be its layer even though in this scenario it is an earlier layer than A\u0027s.",
